WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.240
Phillip: So why is it important to
have product or service knowledge?

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.160
Maddy: Well let’s think about
this by using an example.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.670
Imagine you go to a computer
shop to buy a new laptop.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:16.110
You want it to be able to be powerful enough
for streaming and gaming, to have good

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:21.029
battery life, a cutting-edge graphics card
and a host of other up to date functions.

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:25.760
But when you get to the shop and ask the staff
about the products and which one best meets your

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:31.040
specifications, none of them seem to be able to
answer your questions, and they don’t have any

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.569
real knowledge, only what’s written on the label!

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:37.869
Phillip: In most cases you wouldn’t be very
impressed and would choose to go elsewhere,

00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:40.370
where you can get that advice and service.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.990
This example demonstrates how important it
is that you have the appropriate knowledge

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.670
of the product or services that you provide
when dealing with people outside of your team.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.629
Maddy: So, who are those outside your team?

00:00:55.359 --> 00:00:58.869
These will probably be internal
and external customers.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:05.099
• Internal customers are members of the same
organization as you, who rely on the service

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:10.449
or product being available for them to do
their jobs, such as a salesperson who needs the

00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:12.600
product to be finished, so they can sell it.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.580
• An external customer is someone outside
of your organization, who is interested

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.250
in purchasing your product or service.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:26.010
Phillip: There may also be situations where
superiors within your organization, regulators,

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:30.409
or law enforcement may come to you, to find
out more about your product or service.

00:01:31.939 --> 00:01:35.010
Maddy: Knowledge of your product
and services allows you to give a

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.539
positive impression of your team.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.749
It shows that you and your team
take what they do seriously.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.729
It shows that the team takes the time to
understand what it is they’re doing and how

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.910
it relates to people outside of the team too.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:52.660
This makes the team look more professional,
showing that they’re engaged with their

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.000
work and are enthusiastic about it.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.670
This not only reflects well on how the
team works, but it makes the product

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.030
or service appear more desirable too.

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:06.479
Phillip: Imagine if you were eating
in a restaurant and you asked what the

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.370
specials were for that day, only for
the waiter or waitress to just say,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:11.000
“Oh!

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.330
I never bother to check the
specials, they’re never that good…”

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.580
It would make it look like the team
in that restaurant don’t care about

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:22.659
what they’re doing, that they want to
undermine each other, and it will probably

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.579
make you unlikely to eat there again.

00:02:26.049 --> 00:02:29.490
Maddy: Having good knowledge of your
product or service also makes it more

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.820
likely that internal and external customers
will be able to get what they want.

00:02:34.429 --> 00:02:38.980
If the service or product they enquire about
initially, isn’t suitable for them, wouldn’t

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.260
it be great if you knew that you could supply
one, that was a closer fit, to meet their needs?

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.010
Maybe the product or service is
unavailable, but with your help and

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.500
advice an alternative can be found.

00:02:51.429 --> 00:02:56.200
Phillip: And remember, even if you don’t have
anything suitable for them, the fact that you

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.920
showed knowledge and passion for the product
or service you provide, this will impress

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.910
them and make them more likely to come to
you in the future, if their needs change.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.880
Maddy: It also demonstrates that your team is
made up of people who are engaged with high

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.579
morale, and who care about their job, team,
and the product or service they provide.

00:03:17.289 --> 00:03:23.210
They want to make that experience for the customer
both internal and external, a positive one and

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.020
by having good knowledge you can help to do this.

